# jtech, the deploy target, keyed on its tailnet name.
#
# Read with `ssh-keyscan -t ed25519 jtech` over the tailnet, which needs no
# authentication, and byte-for-byte identical to the key that was previously
# pinned against jtech's public address -- the same sshd host key -- so this is a
# verified pin rather than trust-on-first-use.
#
# Safe to commit where the public address was not: this name resolves only inside
# the tailnet, and a host key is public by construction. The thing worth hiding was
# the origin address of a box that fronts unrelated services behind a Cloudflare
# proxy, not the fact that a machine called jtech has an ed25519 host key.
#
# jtech answers :22 with Tailscale SSH (`remote software version Tailscale`), so
# the connecting node's tailnet identity is the credential and this pin covers the
# host side of that exchange.
jtech ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIL3pyAaFFNm2isipUIj0OAHgAhQhf7WCpi8PmFcorNC3
